      "dom_id": "question-39096",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{3}=-16$ and ${a}_{8}=-51,$ where ${a}_{3}$ and ${a}_{8}$ are the third and eighth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first $10$ terms of the series, expressed using sigma notation?",
      "worked_solution": "Since we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{3}}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-51-(-16)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-35}{5} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${a}_{3}=-16$, using the formula for the $n$th term, we can compute ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ -16 & ={a}_{1}+(3-1)(-7) \\\\ -16 & ={a}_{1}-14 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have ${a}_{1}=-2$ and $d=-7.$ Thus, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-2+(n-1)(-7) \\\\  & =-2-7n+7 \\\\  & =-7n+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the first ten terms can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-7n+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
