      "name": null,
      "problem": "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n1+\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{7}{3}+…+\\frac{37}{3}+13\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The first term ${a}_{1}=1$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =1+(n-1)(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{3}n-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}n+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}n+\\frac{1}{3} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}n & =\\frac{38}{3} \\\\ n & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $19$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=19.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{19}(\\frac{2}{3}n+\\frac{1}{3})\n$$",
